WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:03.550
PAUL NEWSON: Hi.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.860
My name is Paul Newson, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.920
for the Google Cloud platform.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.910
Almost all applications
need to use

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.984
some kind of persistent durable
storage to get their job done.

00:00:13.984 --> 00:00:15.400
This might be to
store information

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.980
about user accounts, images
you want to capture or serve,

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.170
the time series of events
that is happening that you

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.880
want to analyze, or pretty
much any type of data

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.680
you can imagine.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.780
Not surprisingly, the
Google Cloud platform

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.890
can store all these things.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.240
However, because
different applications

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.940
have different
storage requirements,

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.140
the Google Cloud platform
has not one, but several ways

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.540
of storing persistent data.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.900
Today I'm going to introduce
each of the storage options

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.470
in the Google Cloud
platform and talk

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.360
about their different
characteristics

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.070
to give you some
idea of which ones

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.600
you should use for
your application.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.290
Note that depending
on your application,

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:54.340
you might use one or several
of these storage services

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.460
to get the job done.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.660
Let's start our tour in the
Google Developers console.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.510
I should mention that
we are constantly

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.209
improving the Developers
console to add new features

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.740
and improve usability.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.260
So don't be surprised
if the console you see

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.560
looks different from
the one shown here.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.060
You should be able to
perform similar tasks to what

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.850
I demonstrate here
today in future versions

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.790
of the Developer console.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.940
Here we have a new project
I recently created.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.600
In the left navigation menu,
we see the major categories

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.820
of services offered by
the Google Cloud platform,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.620
including Storage.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.770
Under Storage, we see four of
the storage services offered

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.770
in the Google Cloud
platform-- Cloud

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.710
Bigtable, Cloud Datastore,
Cloud SQL, and Cloud Storage.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.470
There is a fifth storage
option that is not listed here,

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:45.590
but is instead under the
Big Data heading-- BigQuery.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.200
BigQuery is both a storage
service and a powerful analysis

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.260
service, which is why it
is listed under Big Data.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.520
We can divide these
five storage services

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.410
into two categories--
structured and unstructured.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.830
If the data you want to store
can be organized into a table

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.560
structure with columns and rows,
then it is structured data.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.449
Examples might be user profile
information, event logs,

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:13.540
sensor measurements, sales
records, or stock trade data.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.080
Structured data comes in
many different shapes, sizes,

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.550
and usage patterns, and
there is a great diversity

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.120
of ways to store it
and interact with it.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.250
So perhaps not
surprisingly, all but one

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.890
of the storage services offered
by the Google Cloud platform

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:29.420
are for structured data.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.260
Cloud SQL, Cloud Datastore,
Cloud Bigtable, and BigQuery

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.690
all store structured data.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.430
The remaining storage
service, Google Cloud Storage,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.440
stores unstructured
data, by which

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.150
I mean that you give Cloud
Storage a sequence of bytes

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.590
to store, a place
you want to store it,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.460
and a name to identify
that sequence of bytes,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.070
and it stores them for you.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.820
Of course, there may
be internal structure

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.150
to that sequence of bytes.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.920
For instance, it
could be a zip archive

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.740
with a table of contents and
individual archives inside

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.510
of it, or it might be a
JPEG image file, which

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.460
has a well-defined format, or
even a text file in CSV format

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.810
organized into rows and
columns, which sounds

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.770
a lot like structured data.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.290
The key is that Cloud
Storage has no insight

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.760
into that internal structure.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.890
It just faithfully
stores and retrieves

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.560
the exact sequence of
bytes you ask it to,

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.410
regardless of any
internal structure

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.230
that may exist in those bytes.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.040
At this point, you might be
thinking this unstructured data

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.570
you're talking about sure
sounds a whole lot like files.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.690
And you'd be exactly
correct, but Cloud Storage

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.260
calls them objects instead
not because they're

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.110
not like files, but
because files are generally

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.010
stored in a file system.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.160
And the file systems
we're used to interacting

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.260
with on modern operating
systems generally

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.210
have a hierarchical structure
and naming conventions

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.420
that Cloud Storage
does not have.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.280
Thus Cloud Storage
calls the place

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.670
you store things a bucket and
the things that you store,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.990
objects.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.760
Let's use the Developer
console to get

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.980
started with Cloud Storage.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.400
This project does not
currently have any buckets.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.180
So let's create one.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.587
First you have to choose
a name for the bucket.

00:04:05.587 --> 00:04:07.420
It's important to note
that bucket names are

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.340
global across all projects.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.810
If you try to create a
bucket with an obvious name,

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.580
such as Test, you will
probably find that the bucket

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.690
name is not available.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.020
As you can see, the
Developer console helpfully

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.519
checks your bucket
name while you're

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.030
typing it to let you
know if it's a legal name

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.890
and if it's available.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.640
Now we have a legal and
available bucket name,

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.060
but we still have a
couple of choices to make.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.010
The first is the class
of storage to use.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.530
Standard storage is the
default, and if you're

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.660
using Cloud Storage as
part of an application,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.790
is almost always
the right choice.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.590
It provides the best latency
and highest availability

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.560
of the storage classes, which
is usually what you want.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.540
If, for some reason, you can't
accept a lower availability

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.800
for the objects you are
storing in this bucket,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.110
you can choose the durable
reduced availability

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.290
class of storage instead.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.180
As the name implies,
objects stored

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.480
with this class of storage are
just as durable as standard,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.710
but have a reduced
availability expectation.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.290
The service level agreement
for the standard storage class

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:12.630
specifies 99.9% availability.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.560
The durable reduced
availability class

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.420
specifies 99%
availability instead.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.640
The trade-off for accepting
the reduced availability

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.370
expectation is a lower price.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:23.857
Pricing is subject to change.

00:05:23.857 --> 00:05:25.690
So you should consult
the current price list

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.560
for details on the cost of the
different classes of storage.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.090
The final class of
storage is nearline.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:33.740
Nearline storage is intended
for archival scenarios

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:35.540
where you do not
expect to access

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.710
the stored objects often.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.920
Nearline storage also carries
a 99% availability SLA.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.640
Nearline storage also has
slightly higher latency

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.590
than standard or durable
reduced availability storage

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.850
with an expectation of a few
seconds to the first byte,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.290
compared with less than a
second to the first byte

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.700
for the other
classes of storage.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.080
Nearline storage is
significantly less expensive

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.810
than standard or durable
reduced availability storage.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:04.010
But there is a surcharge
for accessing your objects.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.820
A good rule of thumb for
when to use near line storage

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.460
is that if you think you will
access an object less than once

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.490
a month, then nearline storage
will probably be the more

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.620
cost effective option.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.850
If you expect to access an
object more than once a month,

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.670
durable reduced
availability storage

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.660
will be more cost effective.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.290
The second choice
is the location

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:26.070
where your data will be stored.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.770
There are two types of locations
available-- multi-regional and

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.090
regional.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.330
Regional locations
correspond to the region

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.680
supported in other
cloud platform services

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.930
such as Compute Engine.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.280
If you primarily intend to
read and write to this bucket

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.870
from Compute Engine instances
in a particular region,

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.140
then setting the location of
the bucket to that same region

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.640
will provide lower latency
and higher throughput

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.540
for that workload.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:56.210
However, by setting the
location to a particular region,

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.070
you restrict the storage
to only that region,

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.270
which can be a disadvantage
if your objects are

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:04.610
going to be accessed from
many different places.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.360
If your objects will be accessed
from many different places,

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.070
then you can choose a
multi-region location

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.610
which restricts the
placement of your data

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:15.350
to a broader geographic area.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:18.690
Currently, the choices are
Asia, the European Union,

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.530
and the United States.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.840
These multi-region
locations can also

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.520
be useful when you want
to ensure your data stays

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.008
within a certain jurisdiction.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.520
With these choices made, you
can now create your bucket.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.850
Here we see our new bucket,
which is, of course, empty.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.415
We could use the developer
console to upload objects.

00:07:39.415 --> 00:07:41.290
But instead, I would
like to quickly show you

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.770
how to use the command line
utility gsutil that comes

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.550
with the Google Cloud SDK.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.320
I have previously installed
the Google Cloud SDK

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.560
and configured it to
point to the project

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.900
and use my credentials.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.230
We can use gsutil to
see the bucket we just

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:59.090
created using the ls command.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.920
gsutil uses a URI
syntax for object names

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.850
where the gs prefix
specifies objects

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.240
stored in Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.460
It can also access files in the
local file system and objects

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.920
stored in Amazon S3 using
the S3 prefix, which

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.520
is useful for
transferring objects

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.070
between Cloud Storage
and the local file system

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:22.560
or between Cloud
Storage and Amazon S3.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.510
Here I will quickly
demonstrate how

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.090
to upload a large number of
objects in a single command.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.490
And we can switch back
to the Developer console,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.200
refresh the window, and see
those objects in our bucket.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.220
Now let's take a quick hands-on
tour of the structured storage

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.980
options starting with Cloud SQL.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:42.640
When we click on Cloud SQL,
we are given the opportunity

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.796
to create a Cloud SQL instance.

00:08:44.796 --> 00:08:46.420
We choose a name for
the instance which

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:48.430
must be unique
within this project,

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:50.740
choose a region in which
to run the instance,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.620
and a size for the instance.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.980
There are lots of additional
configuration options

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.310
that I won't go
into detail on now.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.890
But the one I wanted to point
out is database version.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.210
The important thing to notice
is that you are selecting

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.330
an explicit MySQL version.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.230
This demonstrates a key
point about Cloud SQL.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:11.490
It is a hosted MySQL service.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.200
It is not similar to MySQL.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.040
It is MySQL.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.780
Whatever you do
today with MySQL,

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.530
you can do with Cloud SQL.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:21.070
But Google takes care of
keeping the operating system up

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.810
to date, configuring
application, performing

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.570
backups, and other ops tasks.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.250
To underscore the
point that this really

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.880
is a MySQL server we
started, let's connect

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.420
to it using the MySQL client.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:34.880
Let's create a new
instance we can

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.340
use to run the MySQL client.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.040
Because this instance is
part of the same project,

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:43.090
no additional network
configuration is required.

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:45.610
However, we do need to
make sure we enable access

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.050
to Cloud SQL for this instance.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:51.870
We can do this explicitly
in Access and Security,

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:55.380
or we can allow this instance,
to access all Cloud resources

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.380
that are part of this project.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.810
Since we may use this
instance for more than just

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.970
running the MySQL client,
we will enable access

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:05.700
to all the APIs
with this checkbox.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.430
Once the instance is
created, we can SSH into it

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:12.420
and install the MySQL client
via [? app.get. ?] However, we

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.370
need to make a couple
of configuration changes

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.730
to our Cloud SQL instance
before we can connect to it.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.600
First, we need an IPv4
address to connect to.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.680
Then, we need to tell
Cloud SQL that we're

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.130
OK with connections coming from
the IP address of our Compute

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.670
Engine instance.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.410
In this example, we only want
one Compute Engine instance

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:31.590
to get access.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:34.340
So we specify a network
with a 32-bit prefix,

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.770
which effectively
means a single host.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.930
Finally, we need an
account that allows

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:42.030
us to sign in from our host.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.630
The default root accounts
only work from local host.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.400
So we create a new account
that allows root to sign in

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.510
from our instance's IP address.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:54.150
Now we are ready to connect
to our Cloud SQL instance

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:56.200
using the MySQL client.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.420
At this point, you have
a fully functional MySQL

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:00.950
prompt with which you can
do all the normal stuff

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.860
you would expect to be
able to do from a root

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.850
MySQL prompt such as creating
new databases, creating tables,

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:08.430
and so on.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.250
But now you get the
idea that Cloud SQL

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:13.590
is a fully functional MySQL
database, which is fantastic

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:16.580
if what you want is a low
maintenance MySQL instance.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:20.090
It has everything you expect
from SQL-- a rich query

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:24.550
language, primary and secondary
indexes, asset transactions,

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:28.840
relational integrity, stored
procedures, the works.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.770
If you are familiar with MySQL
or another relational database,

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:35.250
and you value the power of
a full relational database,

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:38.080
and if your scalability
needs are not too great,

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.730
Cloud SQL could be the perfect
solution for your application.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.940
However, the so-called
NoSQL family of databases

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.340
was created to address
some of the issues faced

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:49.860
by relational databases
when maintaining

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.170
relational integrity and full
ACID semantics while attempting

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.740
to attain massive scale in
a cost effective way, which

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.880
brings us to Cloud Datastore.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:01.260
Cloud Datastore was born
as the structured storage

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.110
solution for App
Engine, but is now

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.880
accessible from outside
of App Engine as well.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.970
Cloud Datastore
scales gracefully

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:11.770
from very small database sizes
to very large database sizes.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:15.440
It grows with your application,
both in terms of scale

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.920
and by being flexible when it
comes to schema definition.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:21.370
For those of you who are
accustomed to third normal form

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.350
with full relational
integrity, you

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.860
may find the NoSQL approach
to schema definition

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:28.240
to be a little bit
fast and loose.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:29.650
And you'd be right.

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.940
It is fast, by which I
mean highly scalable.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:36.680
And it is loose, by which I
mean it is highly flexible.

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:38.710
There is no equivalent
to a create table

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.430
statement in Cloud Datastore.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.720
You simply start storing things.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.110
There is no equivalent to
an alter table statement

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:46.450
to add new columns.

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:49.490
You just start storing things
with additional columns.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.941
We can demonstrate this
in the Developers console.

00:12:52.941 --> 00:12:54.690
There are a few important
things to notice

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.400
about what we're doing here.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.830
While this may look a little
like defining a table,

00:12:58.830 --> 00:12:59.990
it's not.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:03.720
What we're doing is storing the
very first entity of its kind

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.290
in our project's data store.

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:09.360
We can then store a second
entity of the same kind,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.120
but we can add new
properties to it that

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.850
were not in the first entity.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:16.510
You'll see when we create a
second entity it knows which

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:19.970
of the first entity's properties
were marked as being indexed

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:22.520
and asks us for a
value for those.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.500
But it does not
demand that we provide

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.950
a value for every property
that was in the first entity.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:30.190
In fact, you can
even create entities

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:32.980
that do not have values for
properties that were previously

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:36.634
identified as being indexed.

00:13:36.634 --> 00:13:38.050
At this point, it
bears mentioning

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:40.430
that just because Cloud
Datastore doesn't require

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.260
you to define your
schema in advance

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.910
doesn't mean you don't need
to think very carefully

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.340
about your data model.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:47.850
You still need to
think carefully

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:49.640
about what you're
planning to store

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:51.670
and how you intend
to retrieve it.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:54.310
But the NoSQL approach means
that when the time comes

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.570
to store additional information
about a kind, which in a SQL

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:00.180
world would mean an alter
table statement to add columns

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.550
to a table, you can
simply start storing

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.650
new entities of that kind with
the additional information

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:07.410
you now require.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.400
You'll need a plan to
either backfill old data

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:12.930
or deal with entities that
are missing that data.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:16.090
But that's true no matter what
your database may look like.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.080
Another thing to notice
is that at no point

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.920
did we tell the Datastore
how many resources should be

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.010
used for our structured data.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:27.190
It is a true no-op solution with
almost no operational overhead,

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:30.510
no need to specify instance
sizes or cluster sizes

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:32.120
or anything of that nature.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.930
Datastore is a great fit
for an application database

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:38.990
that scales from 0 to terabytes
of data as your application

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:40.770
grows in popularity.

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:44.650
However, if your scaling
needs are truly massive,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.200
there is another NoSQL
solution you should

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:49.575
consider-- Cloud Bigtable.

00:14:49.575 --> 00:14:53.150
Cloud Bigtable is a hosted
version of the same Bigtable

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.420
technology that Google
has been using internally

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.370
for over a decade.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.520
In 2006, Google
published a paper

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:01.620
describing Bigtable
which resulted

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.110
in a flurry of activity in
the open source community

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.160
to build similar systems.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:09.150
One of those open source
projects inspired by Bigtable

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:12.730
was HBase, which is part of
the larger Hadoop ecosystem.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:16.290
Cloud Bigtable is exposed
through the HBase API, which

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.130
makes it instantly compatible
with a great deal of existing

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.880
code written for the
Hadoop ecosystem.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.060
But it is a managed
service, which reduces

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.030
your operational overhead.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:27.530
If you know you're
going to be story

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.940
over a terabyte of
structured data,

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.690
if you have a very
high volume of writes,

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:35.440
if you need read and write
latency in the single digit

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:38.690
millisecond range with
strong consistency,

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.080
or if you're already
using HBase and you want

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:44.650
a straightforward migration
path to a managed Cloud Service,

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.870
Cloud Bigtable is something
you should look into.

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.230
Let's create our very own
Cloud Bigtable cluster

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:52.330
to see what that looks like.

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:54.500
We have a couple of choices
to make at this point.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:58.490
First is to choose the zone
in which our cluster will run.

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:00.710
Recall that for Cloud
SQL, we selected a region.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.100
And for Cloud Datastore,
we selected nothing.

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.530
We simply started storing
data and let Cloud Datastore

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:07.760
worry about where it should go.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:11.060
Cloud Bigtable is a lower level
database than both Cloud SQL

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:13.020
and Cloud Datastore,
which is one

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.030
of the reasons it
can scale so high

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.480
and provide such low latency.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:19.380
But with such low
latency guarantees,

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.350
your whole cluster
runs in a single zone.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:25.730
The second choice is how
many nodes to provision.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:27.330
As we increase the
number of nodes,

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:29.230
we increase the
available operations

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:33.180
per second and throughput,
and also, of course, the cost.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:36.740
You can go up to 30 nodes
without even talking to us.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.770
As the developer console
shows, a 30 node cluster

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:45.480
is capable of performing
300,000 operations per second

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:49.550
with a throughput of 300
megabytes per second.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.680
To put that in perspective,
300 megabytes per second

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.080
is about 25 terabytes
per day, which

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:59.630
would add up to about
nine petabytes in a year.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:00.870
That's scale.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.840
Similar to how we
used the native MySQL

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:06.069
client to demonstrate that
Cloud SQL really is MySQL,

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:07.540
I'm going to
demonstrate how we can

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:11.420
use the HBase client to connect
to our Cloud Bigtable cluster.

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:13.560
Here I am using the
Quick Start script

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.950
that is described in the
Cloud Bigtable Quick Start.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.410
Setting up the HBase
client can be tricky,

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:20.910
and the Quick Start
packaging script

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.076
makes sure you have the
right things on your machine

00:17:23.076 --> 00:17:25.089
and understands how to
discover and connect

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:27.474
to your Cloud Bigtable cluster.

00:17:27.474 --> 00:17:28.890
We can see that
we don't currently

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.760
have any tables in our cluster.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:34.710
So we create one,
then add a row to it,

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:38.720
then scan the table to
verify the row is there.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.775
Learning how to model data in
Bigtable and use the HBase API

00:17:41.775 --> 00:17:44.440
is beyond the scope
of this video.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.010
But this short
example serves to show

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:48.500
that Bigtable does
indeed play nicely

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.590
with the HBase toolchain.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:54.340
Given the massive scalability
provided by Cloud Bigtable,

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:56.660
why not make it your
number one choice?

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.660
After all, everyone
wants to be prepared

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.950
for the overwhelming success
of their application.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:04.930
So why not choose the most
scalable database available?

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:07.380
Simply put, Cloud Bigtable
does not effectively

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.680
scale down to small sizes.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.420
The smallest Cloud Bigtable
cluster you can create still

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.490
has three nodes and can
handle 30,000 operations

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:19.080
per second, which is far more
than a small application needs.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.500
This isn't a problem from
a performance standpoint,

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:24.670
but it is a problem from a
cost effectiveness perspective.

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:27.190
These three nodes are
dedicated for your sole use

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:31.140
and cost the same per hour no
matter if you use them or not.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.350
Contrast this with
Cloud Data Storage model

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:35.060
where you pay by the operation.

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:38.380
So when your usage is
small, so is your cost.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:40.080
Each of the structured
storage solutions

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:43.970
we have discussed so far-- Cloud
SQL, Cloud Datastore, and Cloud

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:47.270
Bigtable-- are primarily
operational databases.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:50.230
They're intended to be used
as part of an application.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:51.800
The final structured
storage service

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:53.950
we're going to talk
about is BigQuery, which

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:56.360
is an analytical database.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.560
The power of BigQuery
is its ability

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:03.000
to run SQL queries over
terabytes of data in seconds.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.840
To demonstrate, I will use
one of the several public data

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.250
sets available in BigQuery.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:12.690
This dataset contains 100
billion rows of page view data

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:15.020
from the Wikimedia Foundation.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:17.040
By running this
row count query, we

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.860
can see that there are indeed
more than 100 billion rows

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:21.740
in this table.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.900
Now we're going to scan all
100 billion of those rows,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.360
run a regular expression
against each one,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.500
then aggregate the page
views for each matching row.

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:32.740
And there we have it.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:35.290
We just scanned over
three terabytes of data

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:37.570
in less than a minute.

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:40.110
I hope you've enjoyed this
whirlwind tour of the storage

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:42.560
options in the Google
Cloud platform.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:45.050
We were barely able to
scratch the surface of each

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:46.410
of these great products.

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:48.380
But hopefully you
have a better idea

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:51.000
of which ones might be
best for your application.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

